data.content:
**No Thread.Abort migration is needed here.** Your code only uses `Thread.Start`, `Thread.Join`, and `Thread.Sleep` — these APIs work identically in .NET 8. There are no calls to `Thread.Abort`, no `ThreadAbortException` catch blocks, no `Thread.ResetAbort`, and no `Thread.Interrupt`. It will compile and run correctly as-is on `net8.0`.

---

**Optional modernization** (unrelated to Thread.Abort migration):

You could replace the manual threading with `Parallel.ForEach` or `Task.WhenAll` for cleaner code and better thread-pool usage:

```csharp
public class SimpleWorker
{
    public void RunBatch(List<WorkItem> items)
    {
        Parallel.ForEach(items, item => Process(item));
    }

    private void Process(WorkItem item)
    {
        Thread.Sleep(100);
        item.Result = ComputeResult(item.Input);
    }
}
```

This is purely an improvement for maintainability and resource efficiency — not a requirement for the migration.
